}}{{Politics of Guinea-Bissau}}

Presidential elections were held in [[Guinea-Bissau]] on 18 March 2012 following the death of [[President of Guinea-Bissau|President]] [[Malam Bacai Sanhá]] on 9 January. A run-off was set to be held on 29 April after being postponed by a week as announced by electoral commission chief Desejado Lima Dacosta.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.africareview.com/News/Bissau+run+off+delayed+to+April+29/-/979180/1384960/-/jvdir1/-/index.html |title=Africa Review – Guinea Bissau presidential run off vote delayed to 29 April |publisher=Africareview.com |access-date=15 April 2012 |archive-date=21 March 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200321165336/http://www.africareview.com/News/Bissau%2Brun%2Boff%2Bdelayed%2Bto%2BApril%2B29/-/979180/1384960/-/jvdir1/-/index.html |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="postpone"/> However, after a [[2012 Guinea-Bissau coup d'état|military coup]], the leading candidates were arrested and the election was cancelled. The junta's spokesman then announced plans to hold an election in two years, despite condemnation.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.reuters.com/article/bissau-angola-bauxite-idUSL6E8JNHFM20120823 | work=Reuters | title=Bissau government to review Angola Bauxite deal, calls it unfair | date=23 August 2012 | access-date=5 July 2021 | archive-date=24 September 2015 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924170322/http://www.reuters.com/article/2012/08/23/bissau-angola-bauxite-idUSL6E8JNHFM20120823 | url-status=live }}</ref> [[2014 Guinea-Bissau general election|General elections]] were subsequently held in April 2014.

==Background==
Following the death of [[Malam Bacai Sanhá]] on 9 January 2012, an early presidential elections were scheduled to be held within 90 days, in accordance with the [[constitution of Guinea-Bissau|constitution]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5jQ_g50A9P12C5ScvEpONCJS7PTZQ?docId=CNG.f8db5baad7ae448fa2cbc149e29e6ae8.01 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20121205200059/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5jQ_g50A9P12C5ScvEpONCJS7PTZQ?docId=CNG.f8db5baad7ae448fa2cbc149e29e6ae8.01 |url-status=dead |archive-date=5 December 2012 |title=AFP: Guinea-Bissau leader Malam Bacai Sanha dies in Paris |date=9 January 2012 |access-date=15 April 2012}}</ref>

No president in the history of independent Guinea-Bissau has completed his term in office: Three presidents have been ousted, [[João Bernardo Vieira|one was assassinated]], and [[Malam Bacai Sanhá|another died in office]].<ref name="news24"/>

==Campaign==
[[Prime Minister of Guinea-Bissau|Prime Minister]] [[Carlos Gomes Júnior]] resigned on 10 February to run for the presidency.<ref>[https://www.voanews.com/a/un-mission-pleased-with-peaceful-election-in-guinea-bissau---143271766/180374.html UN Mission Pleased With Peaceful Election in Guinea Bissau]  VOA News, 17 March 2012</ref> A total of nine candidates contested the elections,<ref>[http://africanelections.tripod.com/gw.html Elections in Guinea Bissau] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130115101750/http://africanelections.tripod.com/gw.html |date=15 January 2013 }} African Elections Database</ref> five of whom ran in the [[2009 Guinea-Bissau presidential election|previous elections]] in 2009. Their campaign literature was said to be "largely recycled." Carlos Gomes Júnior and Kumba Ialá were said to be the frontrunners in the election.<ref name="aljaz"/>

Ialá's support base was primarily based on his [[Balanta people|Balanta]] ethnic group. Gomes Júnior had indicated he wanted to reform the armed forces, with which he had a tense relationship.<ref name="aljaz">{{cite web |url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/africa/2012/03/201231844554576763.html |title=Guinea-Bissau counts presidential poll votes – Africa |publisher=Al Jazeera English |access-date=15 April 2012 |archive-date=11 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190411031844/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/africa/2012/03/201231844554576763.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Campaigning for the second round was due to start on 13 April and end on 27 April.<ref name="postpone">{{cite web|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5gvbgLiOwMBZ7IjjF0ZAVFJ31-qhw?docId=CNG.4950c0f67e13086fe38f02331e0333bb.6b1 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130103114836/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5gvbgLiOwMBZ7IjjF0ZAVFJ31-qhw?docId=CNG.4950c0f67e13086fe38f02331e0333bb.6b1 |url-status=dead |archive-date=3 January 2013 |title=AFP: Guinea-Bissau second round vote delayed to 29 April |access-date=15 April 2012}}</ref>

==Conduct==
[[UNIOGBIS]] spokesman Vladimir Monteiro said: "The election was held in a very peaceful manner. In the morning, participation was relatively weak but, all day long, leaders of the electoral body encouraged the people to go and vote, and it seems that people listened and went to vote because the participation finally increased." He also added that the election commission is mandated by the constitution to release the result within 10 days of the election.<ref>{{cite web |author=Peter Clottey |url=https://www.voanews.com/a/un-mission-pleased-with-peaceful-election-in-guinea-bissau---143271766/180374.html |title=UN Mission Pleased With Peaceful Election in Guinea Bissau |publisher=Voice of America |date=18 March 2012 |access-date=15 April 2012 |archive-date=22 March 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120322023349/http://www.voanews.com/english/news/africa/UN-Mission-Pleased-With-Peaceful-Election-in-Guinea-Bissau---143271766.html |url-status=live }}</ref> However, the same night fears of military-linked violence increased with the assassination of the former head of military intelligence, Colonel Samba Diallo, just before midnight at a bar in the national capital of [[Bissau]]. ''[[The Guardian]]'' reported witnesses as saying that soldiers had fired at him and then taken his body away, possibly to a hospital.<ref>{{cite news|author=Afua Hirsch |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2012/mar/18/guinea-bissau-vote-president-military-coup |title=Guinea-Bissau votes to elect president but military overthrow feared |department=World news |work=The Guardian  |access-date=15 April 2012 |location=London |date=18 March 2012}}</ref>

==Results==
No candidate was able to attain a 50% majority in the first round. The leading two candidates, Carlos Gomes Júnior and Kumba Ialá were set to face each other in a runoff election to be held on 22 April.

Five of the first round candidates complained that the poll had been fraudulent despite independent, international observers stating that it was conducted fairly.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-africa-17462109 |title=BBC News – Guinea-Bissau set for presidential run-off |publisher=BBC |date=21 March 2012 |access-date=15 April 2012 |archive-date=24 March 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120324211113/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-africa-17462109 |url-status=live }}</ref>

