==Aftermath==
Despite a peaceful campaign, there were fears of possible violence or a coup d'état if the army did not approve of the winner. UN Secretary General [[Ban Ki-moon]] called for a "peaceful, orderly and transparent" election.<ref name="news24">{{cite web |url=http://www.news24.com/Africa/News/Guinea-Bissau-wraps-up-poll-campaign-20120316 |title=Guinea-Bissau wraps up poll campaign |publisher=News24 |date=16 March 2012 |access-date=15 April 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140810204010/http://www.news24.com/Africa/News/Guinea-Bissau-wraps-up-poll-campaign-20120316 |archive-date=10 August 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Opposition leaders, led by Ialá, called for a boycott of the second round because they considered the election fraudulent,<ref>[http://www.utsandiego.com/news/2012/Mar/22/guinea-bissau-candidate-says-wont-do-vote-runoff/ Guinea-Bissau candidate says won't do vote runoff] Associated Press, 22 March 2012</ref> with Ialá calling for new voter registration to take place and warned against campaigning.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-africa-17492067 |title=BBC News – Kumba Yala boycotts Guinea-Bissau presidential run-off |publisher=BBC |date=23 March 2012 |access-date=15 April 2012 |archive-date=22 March 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200322070232/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-africa-17492067 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.globalpost.com/dispatch/news/regions/africa/120413/guinea-bissau-army-seizes-parts-capital-attempted-coup |title=Guinea-Bissau: Soldiers arrest Prime Minister in apparent coup bid |publisher=GlobalPost |access-date=15 April 2012 |archive-date=16 May 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130516154519/http://www.globalpost.com/dispatch/news/regions/africa/120413/guinea-bissau-army-seizes-parts-capital-attempted-coup |url-status=live }}</ref>

The Director General of the Judicial Police Joao Biague announced that the former head of intelligence, Samba Diallo, was assassinated shortly after the polls closed.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://m.news24.com/kenya/Africa/News/Guinea-Bissau-poll-marred-by-killing-20120320 |title=Guinea-Bissau poll marred by killing |publisher=M.news24.com |date=20 March 2012 |access-date=15 April 2012}}</ref> On 12 April, elements within the [[Politics of Guinea-Bissau#2011 attempted coup|factionalised]] army staged a [[2012 Guinea Bissau coup d'état|coup d'état]], leading to the arrest of both second round candidates, amongst others, by the Military Command and calls for a [[national unity government]].

